Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-533","Network requests and API calls",["Exponential Backoff","Common Use Cases"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-568","Distributed systems communication",["Exponential Backoff","Common Use Cases"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-606","Rate limiting and throttling",["Exponential Backoff","Common Use Cases"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-639","Database connection retries",["Exponential Backoff","Common Use Cases"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-671","Benefits",["Exponential Backoff"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-686","Prevents overwhelming systems under stress",["Exponential Backoff","Benefits"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-733","Allows temporary issues to resolve naturally",["Exponential Backoff","Benefits"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-782","Reduces network congestion",["Exponential Backoff","Benefits"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-813","More efficient than fixed-interval retries",["Exponential Backoff","Benefits"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-860","Example Formula",["Exponential Backoff"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-882","delay = min(max_delay, initial_delay * (base ^ attempt_number))",["Exponential Backoff","Example Formula"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-952","For base=2:",["Exponential Backoff","Example Formula"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-968","1st retry: 1s",["Exponential Backoff","Example Formula","For base=2:"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-987","2nd retry: 2s",["Exponential Backoff","Example Formula","For base=2:"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-1006","3rd retry: 4s",["Exponential Backoff","Example Formula","For base=2:"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-1025","4th retry: 8s",["Exponential Backoff","Example Formula","For base=2:"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-1044","etc.",["Exponential Backoff","Example Formula","For base=2:"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-1054","Note:",["Exponential Backoff","Example Formula"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-1064","The key property of exponential backoff is multiplicative growth of the wait-time:",["Exponential Backoff","Example Formula","Note:"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-1680","Implementation Examples",["Exponential Backoff"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-1710","LangChain/Blog/25/04/17 LangChain Python Improved Content Blocks Retry Logic and More - LangChain's Runnable.with_retry implementation",["Exponential Backoff","Implementation Examples"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-1853","See also",["Exponential Backoff"]],["Programming/Error/Handling/Retry%20Logic/Exponential%20Backoff/","source-block-1868","LangChain/output_parsers/retry/RetryOutputParser",["Exponential Backoff","See also"]],["Programming/Greenfield/","source-block-31","Greenfield coding refers to",[]],["Programming/Greenfield/","source-block-61","the development of a new software application or system from scratch",["Greenfield coding refers to"]],["Programming/Greenfield/","source-block-137","without any constraints imposed by prior work",["Greenfield coding refers to"]],["Programming/Greenfield/","source-block-190","The term implies starting with a \"clean slate,\"",[]],["Programming/Greenfield/","source-block-244","allowing developers to create solutions based on the latest technologies and methodologies without needing to accommodate or work around legacy systems",["The term implies starting with a \"clean slate,\""]],["Programming/Greenfield/","source-block-403","History of the Term:",[]],["Programming/Greenfield/","block-67b5a3d0-4c23-4a36-a89d-cd2d3154f263","The term \"greenfield\" originates from urban planning and real estate, where it describes undeveloped land (often agricultural) that has not been previously built upon.In technology and software development, it gained popularity in the late 20th century as a metaphor for projects that start anew. This contrasts with \"brownfield\" projects (Programming/Legacy Codebase), which involve modifying or integrating with existing systems. The distinction has become essential in discussions of software development practices, particularly in agile methodologies and cloud computing, where the ability to innovate without legacy constraints can lead to more efficient and effective solutions.",["History of the Term:"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-35","Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation",[]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-94","Overview",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-109","A native implementation means a system reimplements functionality entirely within its own runtime and toolchain, without calling out to a foreign environment or tool—such as JavaScript engines or CLI wrappers.",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Overview"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-329","Context",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-343","Many languages and platforms add support for complex tools like diagram rendering, image processing, or parsers by wrapping existing tools written in another language.",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Context"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-515","The term \"native implementation\" arises when there's a desire to avoid such wrappers and instead build or port the feature into the host environment directly.",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Context"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-678","Mermaid rendering provides a concrete example: it's originally implemented in JavaScript, but other languages often want to render Mermaid diagrams without relying on JavaScript or Node.js.",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Context"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-872","Key Principles",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-893","Same-Language Execution – Native implementations execute entirely within the host language’s runtime (e.g. Python, Rust, Go).",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Key Principles"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-1031","No Foreign Process Calls – Native implementations do not shell out to tools like mmdc, embed a browser, or depend on headless Node-based infrastructure.",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Key Principles"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-1196","Self-Sufficiency – All logic for parsing, layout, and rendering is reimplemented or directly handled by the host language’s libraries.",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Key Principles"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-1343","Mechanism",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-1359","Native Implementation",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Mechanism"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-1389","Reimplement the tool’s grammar, parsers, and layout engines.",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Mechanism","Native Implementation"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-1457","Use native graphics libraries to generate output (SVG, PNG, etc.).",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Mechanism","Native Implementation"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-1529","Avoid runtime dependencies on external toolchains.",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Mechanism","Native Implementation"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-1585","Wrapper-Based Approach",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Mechanism"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-1616","Generate intermediate output (e.g., Mermaid .mmd text).",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Mechanism","Wrapper-Based Approach"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-1679","Invoke a command-line tool like mmdc or spin up a headless browser.",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Mechanism","Wrapper-Based Approach"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-1754","Capture and return rendered output from the subprocess.",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Mechanism","Wrapper-Based Approach"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-1815","Examples",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-2336","Misconceptions",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-2357","“Embedding a tool makes it native.” Embedding Node.js still relies on external execution and does not count as native.",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Misconceptions"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-2488","“Code generation is enough.” Generating Mermaid syntax (.mmd) is not rendering; native refers to processing and rendering the diagram.",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Misconceptions"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-2637","Related",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-2651","Mermaid/mmdc",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Related"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-2672","Mermaid CLI (mmdc) GitHub",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Related","Mermaid/mmdc"]],["Programming/Implementation/Native/","source-block-2750","MermaidJS Documentation",["Native Implementation vs Wrapper Rendering Explanation","Related","Mermaid/mmdc"]],["Programming/Language/","source-block-69","Programming Languages (PL)",[]],["Programming/Language/","source-block-100","Topics in this namespace cover programming languages and closely related ideas.",["Programming Languages (PL)"]],["Programming/Language/","source-block-187","Paradigms",["Programming Languages (PL)"]],["Programming/Language/","source-block-203","Software/Paradigm/Functional",["Programming Languages (PL)","Paradigms"]],["Programming/Language/","source-block-240","Software/Paradigm/Object-Oriented",["Programming Languages (PL)","Paradigms"]],["Programming/Language/","source-block-282","Programming/Language/Concept/Declarative vs Imperative",["Programming Languages (PL)","Paradigms"]],["Programming/Language/","source-block-345","See also",["Programming Languages (PL)"]],["Programming/Language/","source-block-360","Programming/Language/General Purpose",["Programming Languages (PL)","See also"]],["Programming/Language/C/11/","source-block-74","The C Programming Language, version 11 (2011/04; newer versions)",[]],["Programming/Language/C/11/","source-block-147","see-also",["The C Programming Language, version 11 (2011/04; newer versions)"]],["Programming/Language/C/11/","source-block-166","CLang",["The C Programming Language, version 11 (2011/04; newer versions)","see-also"]],["Programming/Language/Concept/Declarative%20vs%20Imperative/","source-block-158","Declarative vs Imperative",[]],["Programming/Language/Concept/Declarative%20vs%20Imperative/","source-block-196","Overview",["Declarative vs Imperative"]],["Programming/Language/Concept/Declarative%20vs%20Imperative/","source-block-211","Programming paradigms can be broadly grouped by whether code describes what to compute or how to compute it.",["Declarative vs Imperative","Overview"]],["Programming/Language/Concept/Declarative%20vs%20Imperative/","source-block-336","Declarative code expresses the desired outcome or constraint, leaving the runtime or compiler to figure out the execution steps — SQL queries, HTML markup, and CSS rules are canonical examples.",["Declarative vs Imperative","Overview"]],["Programming/Language/Concept/Declarative%20vs%20Imperative/","source-block-540","Imperative code specifies an explicit sequence of commands that mutate state to arrive at a result — C, shell scripts, and most mainstream loops are imperative at their core.",["Declarative vs Imperative","Overview"]],["Programming/Language/Concept/Declarative%20vs%20Imperative/","source-block-725","The distinction matters because declarative code trades control over execution for readability, correctness guarantees, and optimization opportunities; imperative code trades those for precise control over performance and side effects.",["Declarative vs Imperative","Overview"]],["Programming/Language/Concept/Declarative%20vs%20Imperative/","source-block-973","Context",["Declarative vs Imperative"]],["Programming/Language/Concept/Declarative%20vs%20Imperative/","source-block-987","The terms come from linguistics: a declarative sentence states a fact; an imperative sentence issues a command. The analogy is apt — a SQL SELECT states what rows we want; a loop with conditionals commands how to find them.",["Declarative vs Imperative","Context"]],["Programming/Language/Concept/Declarative%20vs%20Imperative/","source-block-1219","Wikipedia characterizes declarative programming as \"a programming paradigm … that expresses the logic of a computation without describing its control flow\" (Wikipedia: Declarative programming) and imperative programming as a paradigm that \"uses statements that change a program's state\" (Wikipedia: Imperative programming).",["Declarative vs Imperative","Context"]],["Programming/Language/Concept/Declarative%20vs%20Imperative/","source-block-1666","Most real languages are multi-paradigm: Python, JavaScript, and Kotlin support both styles; the question is usually which style dominates a given component or layer.",["Declarative vs Imperative","Context"]]],"format_version":5}